    "q-95742": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95742",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "$$\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "$$\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{0}+{e}^{-0}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+{2}^{-1}}{2}-\\frac{1+1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
