    "q-96265": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96265",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\,\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identities to express the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)+\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}}+\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4-{4}^{-1}}+\\frac{2}{2-{2}^{-1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{15}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
