    "q-97309": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97309",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "$$\n\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
