    "q-138954": {
      "dom_id": "step-q138954",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "$$\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "$$\n\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x})}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}{u}^{-3}\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}\\,{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}\\,{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{32}+\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{175}{288}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
